I'd keep it a deauthentication, as it is. That path already does the security part: `ContextListener` drops the firewall's token, `RememberMeListener` clears the remember-me cookie on `TokenDeauthenticatedEvent`, and `RememberMeAuthenticator` won't log back in from a cookie being cleared in the same request. That's also what happens on a password change. Invalidating would additionally log the user out of other firewalls sharing the session, which this provider has no say over, and `CheckRefreshedUserEvent` asks for side-effect-free listeners.

What I'd add is a dedicated `AuthenticationException` subclass instead of a plain one, so that an app wanting parity with its front-channel logout (`invalidate_session` defaults to true there) can do it from `TokenDeauthenticatedEvent::getException()`:

```php
if ($event->getException() instanceof OidcSessionEndedException) {
    $event->getRequest()->getSession()->invalidate();
}
```

